  doi: "[DOI to be assigned upon publication]"
  abstract: "Publication bias remains a critical threat to the validity of meta-analytic findings, yet existing detection and correction methods each have unique limitations. This paper presents a comprehensive, validated Python implementation of seven complementary publication bias assessment methods: Egger's test, Begg's test, Trim-and-Fill, PET-PEESE with bootstrap confidence intervals, Copas sensitivity analysis, Vevea-Hedges selection model, and the novel MAIVE (Meta-Analysis Instrumental Variable Estimator) approach. Through extensive Monte Carlo simulations (144,000 runs across 144 conditions), we demonstrate that method performance depends critically on meta-analysis characteristics, particularly heterogeneity. MAIVE shows optimal performance with high heterogeneity (I² > 50%), while PET-PEESE excels with low heterogeneity (I² < 25%). All implementations are validated against R metafor and applied to five real-world meta-analyses. We provide evidence-based guidelines for method selection and an open-source interactive dashboard to facilitate adoption of best practices in publication bias assessment."
